[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Routing, Endpoint Filters, and Minimal APIs vs Controllers in .NET"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Modern .NET provides two distinct paradigms for building HTTP APIs: Controllers and Minimal APIs. Understanding how routing works, how to secure endpoints using filters, and when to use each approach is essential for backend architecture design."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Minimal APIs vs Controllers"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Controllers rely on the MVC pattern, inheriting from ControllerBase and utilizing attribute routing. They are heavily structured and suited for large enterprise applications with heavy separation of concerns."},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Minimal APIs are designed for lightweight, fast endpoints with minimal boilerplate. They map routes directly to lambda expressions or static methods inside Program.cs or extension files."},{"order":6,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Controller-Based Implementation"},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"[ApiController]
[Route("api/[controller]")]
public class ProductsController : ControllerBase
{
    [HttpGet("{id}")]
    public IActionResult GetProduct(int id)
    {
        return Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Sample Product" });
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Minimal API Implementation"},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);
var app = builder.Build();

app.MapGet("/api/products/{id}", (int id) => {
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Sample Product" });
});

app.Run();"},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Endpoint Filters in Minimal APIs"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"While Controllers use Action Filters, Authorization Filters, and Middleware, Minimal APIs introduce Endpoint Filters. Endpoint filters implement the IEndpointFilter interface, allowing you to intercept, validate, and modify requests and responses."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"public class LoggingFilter : IEndpointFilter
{
    public async ValueTask<object?> InvokeAsync(
        EndpointFilterInvocationContext context,
        EndpointFilterDelegate next)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Executing request: {context.HttpContext.Request.Path}");
        var result = await next(context);
        Console.WriteLine("Request executed successfully.");
        return result;
    }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Applying Filters to Routes"},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"app.MapGet("/api/secure-data", () => "Protected Data")
   .AddEndpointFilter<LoggingFilter>();"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Summary of Key Differences"},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Controllers offer robust convention-based architecture, ideal for large teams."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Minimal APIs have faster startup times, lower memory overhead, and less boilerplate."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Endpoint filters replace traditional action filters for Minimal APIs, providing strong typing and clean pipeline integration."}]
